# Vauxhall C-type Prince Henry two-seater, 1910-1915.
#
# The C-type and the Prince Henry are ONE car: C10 is the works chassis and
# engine code and Prince Henry is the model name. Announced on 23 April 1910
# and catalogued from the end of 1911, it began as a 3-litre and became the
# 4-litre in late 1912. The open two-seater is modelled.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vauxhall_C-type
#
# Dimensions: wheelbase 2.972 m (117 in) for 1911 and 3.048 m (120 in) for
# 1913; length 4.039 m (159 in) or 4.115 m (162 in), depending on body.
# Width and height were not published, being body-dependent, and are
# estimated from the photograph.
#
# Shape reference: the side image is NOT a true level profile: it is about
# 15-20 degrees off the side axis and the museum label partly covers the front
# wheel. The flank is estimated within the published dimensions and the
# limitation is recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no Vauxhall colour list was found. Black, British
# Racing Green, Maroon and Grey are period finishes. Weights 10, 9, 8, 7,
# total 34, provisional.
#
# Speed 120 km/h. Comfort 25/368, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 25. Classes 2,3.
# Dates: intro April 1910; retires staggered 1928-1932 as a road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-c-type.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/vauxhall-c-type.dae. References:
# citycars/references/vauxhall-c-type/ (gitignored).
#
# Accepted deviations: the width, the height, the flank behind the museum label
# and the rear treatment are estimated. The bonnet flutes and the folded hood
# are simplified. The bonnet is painted rather than the polished finish of
# the surviving museum car.


obj=citycar
name=VauxhallCType(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=10
intro_year=1910
intro_month=4
retire_year=1932
retire_month=6
speed=120
class=2,3
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=368

